#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13f9ed67-24d8-5d41-8f82-1296abc57f12" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Double-Integral Formula for Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "4031c8a9-0559-5d8d-a1a5-6e3402c19b27" :question/math-academy-id "e-26133" :question/problem "Consider the solid enclosed between the paraboloids ![](images/91/9145bfbf3c349359595396d06807191dc666c03558e5943e18d6a356d3b52e80.png) and ![](images/98/98d18317cb2e69f489be66c8a4244e90327ed05aa0de5633fb6c461d81936a89.png) whose volume can be written in terms of double integrals, as follows:\n![](images/7e/7ebb2715de5217f0bd211be342bd8b64026a641254d79e7dde54606c712f0af3.png)\n\nFind ![](images/cd/cd9dbda80a5f41e2eba8b5440f42eddc12015a003e558ea26fd512cf1827a7a3.png) ![](images/54/54827258e24002d07ac1bc4468c8a8f81fa9ee932f102790093989c200860788.png) and ![](images/1b/1b0eaf7c23571a3806b1fdbcb5badeb8127cefe1f5d71ea8d1ea426aa2e0fbff.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the ![](images/33/3397de904d08c80f853cc9ad930c0792c5f6033aa1409655c4623fe3a0edfc86.png)-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n![](images/de/de82f740c53922156a55045b891d73187af742855d8687380f2147468b6625a4.png)\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the ![](images/b7/b7058c5c6e450acdb37c5e67a76139fe8c17abcc4003b200af69d62540050db9.png)-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region:\n![](images/08/081a487a9615ca41e08647729d379a07b4c63e856d8db5d20357ee6e379f9e56.png)\nwhere ![](images/59/5990121d9274012f291f6d3622d0ba077bd009023969faa36b1058d05f4e1962.png) is the interior of the circle ![](images/ea/eab35e9389dfcc153b3ff22e36ff359595c386b3733d61589a04807c25bc56cf.png)\n\nAs a result, the volume of ![](images/e7/e72f4ff500bcbe3f2392425e3b4b777888f579ae6ece9546ca0dae0c08c33493.png) is given by\n![](images/2d/2d850bd7a9cc12a8143f7ee1a9803e4d6ea98ae1c22cfc56e1c5ce989b512f94.png)\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of ![](images/83/837e84cb3633847bb8928cfb823f7c20756bd92362517e5411a0e77684da47a8.png) consists of\n\n- the lower part ![](images/5e/5e020d44aa4de1a4a3c561677f7b0a46ffc9a6b5f1a670fdb4d6ec93f837bf15.png) and\n\n- the upper part ![](images/67/674107266131d1e4e7e3b7147c7eab59e380cbb30c04c44f8942b0ea688973ac.png)\n\nfor ![](images/54/542abe7b3c7cdba70586108ad546acee635ad90d16326b8bad3f7ba430de3f08.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/3c/3c8cf015e3b1944ce18fa83f77a17912afc696e485dc6557760a1d78bfd81e3c.png)\n\nSo,\n![](images/1b/1bc658f161ad3523eaf0a263af0a9b86cb596483fe0f20f6088af1f366ff205f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb891682-26ae-541c-a92d-a79756d1714e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Given in Set Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "7dcea6be-fd16-5312-b50c-157bafcc2d65" :question/math-academy-id "e-8189" :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\Big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{2y}, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq yz \\Big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}{\\int }_{0}^{yz}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}[x]_{x=0}^{x=yz}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}yz\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2}{(\\sqrt{2y})}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8-1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43198aae-a4dd-5b79-803a-1bb625a96355" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "b0ad2f24-6f0c-5d66-9312-5e41840b4fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8190" :question/problem "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\n \n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](images/75/75a76a2dbc34c889335e66623e12b686eb9a255ee0d67f9be0e3792f2650360c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e19ffe6a0555851c2b05526272beb21dbcdc082a23a2e11809c661666efdf133.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(4-x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((4-x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6-2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[6x-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(6(1)-{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c89813f-95aa-50b9